WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:10.080
While working as the Archivist of my collection&nbsp;
of curiosities, I’ve researched a lot of cryptids.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:17.040
Famous monsters like Bigfoot and the Yeti, but&nbsp;
also lesser-known creatures like the Sheepsquatch…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:23.680
and the Loveland Frogman… As a methodical&nbsp;
archivist, I have to admit that a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:28.880
the evidence for such creatures — as amazing&nbsp;
as they are — can be somewhat questionable…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:35.040
Indeed, there’s a long and frankly hilarious&nbsp;
history of people trying and failing to fake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:41.360
the existence of cryptids to the masses. So today,&nbsp;
I’ll be unveiling some of the greatest historical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.880
cryptid hoaxes. And while the monsters on this&nbsp;
list are fake, the real stories behind their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:52.800
fabrication are often just as outlandish as the&nbsp;
creatures themselves — involving lies, double&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.880
crossing, and ridiculous amounts of money down the&nbsp;
drain. So, let’s shine a light on these stories…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:07.520
In the summer of 1937, reports of giant footprints&nbsp;
on the shores of Nantucket shocked the tiny U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:14.400
town. Photographs of what appeared to be the&nbsp;
tracks of a large, unknown animal soon followed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.600
Stories spread that a sea-serpent, right out&nbsp;
of ancient myth, had come ashore somewhere on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:25.840
the island. A few days later, an account came in&nbsp;
that a gigantic monster was spotted on the beach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:32.960
and huge crowds gathered to investigate… And… they&nbsp;
were greeted by this. A sea serpent to be sure…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:40.800
but clearly not a real one. This hilariously fake&nbsp;
creature was the creation of this guy: Tony Sarg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:46.720
an early pioneer in puppetry who had orchestrated&nbsp;
the whole thing — footprints included — to get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:52.960
town and himself into the news. Although even at&nbsp;
the time people obviously weren’t fooled by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:59.040
giant inflatable monster once they actually saw&nbsp;
it, Sarg’s self-promotion plan was a success.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:03.680
Later that year, he’d actually end up&nbsp;
designing the first balloons for the Macy’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:10.480
Day Parade — which his fake Sea Serpent also flew&nbsp;
in. The Nantucket Sea Serpent is a pretty amusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:17.840
and harmless hoax all in all… But not all hoaxes&nbsp;
have such pleasant backstories. In New York in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:25.440
early 1840s, a reported 10,000 pamphlets swept the&nbsp;
city claiming the body of a real-life mermaid had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:31.440
been found and was going on display. The location&nbsp;
of this supposed unveiling was a museum at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:37.040
corner of Broadway and Park Row, which just&nbsp;
so happened to be owned by one P.T. Barnum:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:42.880
the infamous showman, conman and all-around&nbsp;
questionable dude. And this mermaid was right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:47.840
out of his scheming playbook— though advertised&nbsp;
as a beautiful being out of classical myth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:53.040
the actual specimen people would see was a&nbsp;
strange, shriveled creature that horrified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:58.240
onlookers. This is because Barnum’s mermaid&nbsp;
was, of course, not a supernatural creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:04.800
but a hoax. The object was in fact composed of the&nbsp;
body and torso of a taxidermied monkey attached to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:10.480
the back half of a fish — in a pretty twisted&nbsp;
attempt to create a natural looking lifeform.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:15.840
While the scientific community at large debunked&nbsp;
the specimen (multiple times), the mermaid was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:22.240
huge success for Barnum, with people from all over&nbsp;
coming to examine it. The hoax met its end when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:28.000
was likely destroyed in one of the many fires that&nbsp;
destroyed other parts of Barnum’s collections.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:35.600
And so, the mermaid scam was no more... But&nbsp;
other cryptid hoaxes were far more enduring.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:42.000
Perhaps no single faked image has influenced&nbsp;
more people than this famous 1934 photograph&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.600
that supposedly captures the one and only Loch&nbsp;
Ness Monster. This photo is a huge part of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:52.960
launched the supposed sea monster into popular&nbsp;
culture, and led to countless follow up articles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:58.480
and other alleged sightings. It wasn’t until much&nbsp;
later that the picture was officially exposed as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:05.280
a fake… and the tale behind why it was taken is&nbsp;
truly ridiculous. The story of the hoax begins in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:12.080
1933, when the newspaper ‘The Daily Mail’ hired&nbsp;
one Marmaduke Wetherell — pictured here looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:18.160
‘extremely normal’ — to track down the beast. A&nbsp;
British actor turned eccentric big game hunter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:23.440
Wetherell reported a series of footprints&nbsp;
belonging to the creature. Yes, footprints.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:27.440
At this time, the concept of the Loch Ness&nbsp;
Monster being fully aquatic actually wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:32.800
completely in the public consciousness, and a lot&nbsp;
of supposed ‘sightings’ from the era involve the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:38.320
creature lumbering across dry land. Wetherell’s&nbsp;
footprints were ridiculed by the Daily Mail when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:43.840
it turned out he had faked them by pressing the&nbsp;
taxidermied leg of a hippo into the sand. Looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:49.360
to get back at the newspaper, Wetherell paid his&nbsp;
son-in-law to take a photo of a wooden dinosaur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:55.520
head glued to a toy submarine. From a distance,&nbsp;
this hoax made for a decently convincing monster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:02.160
and the rest is history… Not all cryptid hoaxes&nbsp;
were so inexpensive to produce, however. Others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:08.400
like the petrified Cardiff Giant, were extremely&nbsp;
costly and complex undertakings in deception.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:14.080
The grand illusion began two years before the&nbsp;
mysterious giant was unveiled when an American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:20.640
named George Hull hired a German sculpture in&nbsp;
secret to carve a ten foot, or 3-meter-long block&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:27.120
of stone into the likeness of a giant. He then&nbsp;
transported the giant via railroad to the farm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:33.200
of his cousin, where he buried it. Nearly a year&nbsp;
later, Hull hired two men to build a well where he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:38.880
knew his fake petrified giant had been buried.&nbsp;
In total, this elaborate plan likely cost over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:46.080
$51,000 in today’s money. But Hull soon made his&nbsp;
money back, as people from across the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:51.520
scrambled to take a look at the supposed giant.&nbsp;
The giant drew such crowds that another conman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:57.280
hired someone to covertly model the giant’s shape&nbsp;
in wax and create a plaster replica. That conman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:03.760
was… P.T. Barnum again, showing that guy really&nbsp;
was everywhere. Once Barnum started making money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:10.640
off his giant, Hull tried to sue Barnum in court,&nbsp;
calling his giant a fake. Reportedly, the judge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:16.880
told Hull to get his giant to swear on its own&nbsp;
genuineness if he wanted a favorable ruling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:22.000
In the end, both giants were revealed as&nbsp;
fakes in court. Quite the series of twists.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:29.600
Other equally wild hoaxes emerge from much more&nbsp;
peaceful backgrounds. The Cottingley Fairies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:35.760
appear in infamous photographs taken in 1917&nbsp;
near the small English village of Cottingley.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:40.400
These photographs feature what appear to be&nbsp;
various fairies interacting with two young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:46.800
cousins — and are extremely bold compared to many&nbsp;
other hoaxes by not being blurry in the slightest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:52.080
These photographs had a profound effect on the&nbsp;
public, which at the time was experiencing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:57.520
rise in interest in psychic phenomena in part as&nbsp;
an escape from the horrors of the First World War.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:03.200
Sir Arthur Conan Doyle — most famous as the&nbsp;
author of the Sherlock Holmes stories — was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:08.960
a particularly passionate advocate that the&nbsp;
photos were genuine. Many more were skeptical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:14.640
however, as close examination of the photos made&nbsp;
it seem pretty clear the ‘fairies’ were actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:20.480
made of paper positioned so they appeared to be&nbsp;
interacting with the children. While believing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:25.360
photos were real might have been a far more&nbsp;
appealing option, interest soon died down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:30.400
and eventually the two cousins admitted that the&nbsp;
photographs were cardboard cutouts copied from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:36.800
popular children’s books of the time. Nonetheless,&nbsp;
it’s a fascinating moment of history. Not all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:42.400
hoaxes were debunked so easily, however. The&nbsp;
Piltdown Man is perhaps the greatest hoax on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:47.920
list in terms of how long it was accepted by the&nbsp;
scientific community. Though not a cryptid in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:53.760
traditional sense, this fossil supposedly belonged&nbsp;
to a missing link between apes and humans. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:08:00.000
tale of this missing link begins in 1912, when&nbsp;
Charles Dawson — an amateur scientist who should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.040
not be confused with the way cooler Charles Darwin&nbsp;
— claimed to have found a skull that would fill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:11.360
a supposed gap in paleontology regarding human&nbsp;
origins. The majority of scholars in the emerging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:17.040
field believed his finding to be legitimate, and&nbsp;
reconstructions of the Piltdown Man became famous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:22.720
Nationalism and cultural prejudice played a&nbsp;
role in this, as the Piltdown Man satisfied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:28.000
the expectations of white British scientists that&nbsp;
the earliest humans must have emerged in Europe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:33.200
Even at the time, however, more thorough&nbsp;
paleontologists were skeptical from the outset,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:38.720
with some suggesting the skull was actually just&nbsp;
the cranium of a human stuck to the jawbone of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:44.160
an orangutan. This assertion turned out to be&nbsp;
exactly correct, and was proved definitively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:50.160
in 1953, with Charles Dawson being the likely&nbsp;
culprit behind the forgery. From the outset,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:55.840
the missing-link concept of the Piltdown man was&nbsp;
grounded in an outdated understanding of evolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:01.840
as something strictly linear and progressive.&nbsp;
Our modern phylogenetic tree of hominid evolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:07.840
is way, way more complex: with there being no&nbsp;
solitary ‘missing link’ between anatomically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:14.000
modern humans and our anthropoid ancestors. The&nbsp;
Piltdown Man is therefore an even more obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:19.920
hoax in retrospect... And the last hoax on our&nbsp;
list is something more harmless, and quite a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:26.880
more recent. The Pacific Northwest Tree Octopus is&nbsp;
a cryptid from the early internet age. It emerged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:32.960
on the scene with this website encouraging readers&nbsp;
to help save the endangered, tree-dwelling octopus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:39.200
species of the temperate rainforests in North&nbsp;
America. The Tree Octopus is obviously just a fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:44.720
joke, but thanks to the many doctored photographs&nbsp;
on display, a lot of people thought this was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:50.640
real animal. This website actually led to early&nbsp;
discussions on how easily the internet can trick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:56.880
people into believing false information — an idea&nbsp;
that is commonplace now. The creator of this hoax,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:02.640
who goes by the pseudonym Lyle Zapato, is an&nbsp;
early mastermind of surrealist internet humor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:07.600
and I’ve dropped a link to his site and social&nbsp;
media in the description below. Because out of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:14.560
these cryptids, the tree octopus is definitely the&nbsp;
most fun, and my personal favorite… And with that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:20.080
I’ve covered some of craziest hoax stories I’ve&nbsp;
found while doing research for the archive. And if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:25.760
there are other cryptid hoaxes that you’re&nbsp;
a fan of, let me know in the comments. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:31.120
thanks for watching. If you enjoyed this entry,&nbsp;
please lend your support and like, subscribe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:45.840
and hit the notification icon to stay up to date&nbsp;
on all things Curious. See you in the next video.

